# SYNOPSIS

```usage
vx bench [--runs n] [--profile name,...] [--all] [--record [--accept]]
         [--enforce] [--compare ref]
vx bench --zircon [--rounds n] [--only config,...] [--logs dir]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`vx bench` measures the budgets the kernel and the services print as `budget name=... value=... unit=... [limit=...] [target=...]` lines, and judges them.
It needs KVM (`/dev/kvm`) and refuses to run without it: timings under TCG are not judged.

It builds `amd64`, then boots its image <n> times (5 by default) in each of the profiles the change risks.
There are five: `full`, the command line as built with every self-test running; `lean`, the same with `croi.selftest=off`; `services`, the launcher and its services, from which only the `services.`, `node.` and `fs.` budgets are taken; `cpu`, the cpu node with boot-cpu's terminal calling it through port 27119, from which only the `remote.` budgets are taken: the first call after the boot, a 64 MiB copy from `/mnt/term` and a warm boot's return (vx-remote(1)); and `tracker`, boot-tracker's desktop with Tracker, from which only the `tracker.` budgets are taken: its warm listing of a 100,000-entry folder (tracker(4)).
The change is the tree's difference from `main` (from <ref> with `--compare`), mapped through `docs/risk.tsv` as `vx test --changed` maps it: a change that risks `boot-lean` or `boot-smoke` boots `lean`, one that risks `boot-launch` boots `services`, one that risks `boot-cpu` boots `cpu`, and one that names `boot-tracker` boots `tracker`; one that risks the whole suite boots `lean`, `services` and `cpu`.
`full` boots only when named or with `--all`, as does every profile with `--record`, and so does `tracker` when the change doesn't name `boot-tracker`.
When the change risks none, nothing boots.
A profile whose image the build lacks is skipped.
For each budget it takes the median and judges it against the median of the last five runs recorded in `bench/history/<host>.tsv`, where <host> is the machine's host name.
A budget regresses when it rises past both the fraction and the floor that `tools/bench/lib/Rules.swift` sets for its name, or when it passes its own `limit`.
The report goes to the terminal and to `bench/out/report.md`; each boot's console is in `bench/out/logs/`.
It also records the load average before and after, and says so when the machine was busy (a load above a quarter of the CPUs): a regression then may be noise.

By default the bench is advisory and exits 0 whatever it finds, because the machine is shared.

: `--runs` <n>
  Boot <n> times (at least 1) per profile.
: `--profile` <name>,...
  Boot these profiles, whatever the change risks.
: `--all`
  Boot all four.
: `--enforce`
  Exit 1 if any budget regressed or passed its limit.
  Use it only on a quiet machine.
: `--record`
  Append the medians to the history file, if the machine was not busy, the tree is committed, and nothing regressed.
  Otherwise it says why it did not.
: `--accept`
  With `--record`, record despite a busy machine, a dirty tree or a regression.
  A regression recorded this way is a decision to make in the open.
: `--compare` <ref>
  Compare this tree with the commit <ref>.
  It makes a git worktree of <ref> in `bench/out/compare/<sha>`, builds it, and alternates boots of the two so that both see the same machine.
  A boot of either tree that doesn't finish is left out, and said so, rather than ending the comparison.
  It prints a table of the median of each budget in both, the change, and the spread over this tree's runs, and writes it to `bench/out/compare.md`.
  Nothing is judged or recorded.
: `--zircon`
  Boot the kernel's `m2` budgets in four configurations interleaved, in rounds (`--rounds`, 6 by default; `--only` names the configurations, comma separated; `--logs` is a directory for the consoles): `croi`, `croi-nopoll` (idle CPUs halt at once), `zircon` and `zircon-nomitig`.
  The Zircon ones run the parity bench of `tools/zircon-bench`, which needs a Fuchsia tree (`tools/zircon-bench/README.md`).
  The result is each budget's median over the rounds with its range.

# FILES

: `bench/history/<host>.tsv`
  The recorded medians, in seconds, or counts.
: `bench/out/`
  The report, the comparison and the consoles.

